Note on Maxima's own bundled sub-packages
------------------------------------------
Maxima's own upstream source tree bundles a number of further
sub-packages under `share/`, `interfaces/`, and elsewhere, some of which
carry licenses distinct from the plain GPL-2 at the root of the Maxima
source tree. This was previously only spot-checked for `share/logic`;
it has since been swept more broadly by (1) downloading and
checksum-verifying the pinned maxima-5.46.0.tar.gz (against the same
MAXIMA_SHA256 `configure`/`tools/versions.sh` pin), (2) finding every
COPYING/LICENSE-named file anywhere under `share/` and `interfaces/`,
and (3) grepping the same two trees for common non-GPL license markers
("Redistribution and use", "public domain", "MIT License", "BSD
License", etc.) to catch files that assert a different license inline
without a standalone COPYING file. This is still not a literal
per-file audit of every one of the thousands of files in those trees
(see the `share/contrib` note below), but it is a real, repeatable
sweep rather than a single spot-check, and it surfaced the following:

* `share/logic/COPYING`, `interfaces/emacs/imaxima/COPYING`, and
  `interfaces/xmaxima/Tkmaxima/COPYING.tcl` are all, on inspection, the
  plain GNU General Public License, version 2 -- the same license as
  the rest of Maxima (just without the Maxima-specific
  dedication/export-control preamble that precedes the license text in
  Maxima's own top-level COPYING file), so none of these are actually a
  *different* license in practice.
* Maxima's own top-level `LICENSES/` directory already documents one
  genuine exception itself: `LICENSES/getopt-license.txt` states that
  `lisp-utils/getopt.lisp` (Copyright (C) 2003 Kevin M. Rosenberg) is
  under a 3-clause-BSD-style license, not GPL-2.
* `share/minpack/disclaimer` states that the MINPACK numerical routines
  under `share/minpack` (translated from Fortran, Copyright (C) 1999
  University of Chicago, as operator of Argonne National Laboratory)
  carry their own permissive, 3-clause-BSD-style license, not GPL-2.
  The other bundled/translated Fortran numerical libraries alongside it
  under `share/` (`lapack`, `lbfgs`, `odepack`, `hompack`, `cobyla`,
  `colnew`, `fftpack5`) are likewise derived from third-party numerical
  code (LAPACK, ODEPACK, HOMPACK, COBYLA, COLNEW and FFTPACK5
  respectively) that upstream is not GPL-2 either (typically permissive
  or public-domain), but unlike `share/minpack` none of them ship an
  equivalent disclaimer/license file of their own inside Maxima's
  source tree recording that.
* `interfaces/xmaxima/ObjTcl/Object.tcl` (Copyright (C) 1994 Lawrence
  Berkeley Laboratory) carries its own permissive BSD-style license
  (with an advertising clause) inline, distinct from GPL-2.
* `share/contrib/Eulix/Eulix.html` states that one file (a stylesheet)
  is placed in the public domain, and `share/contrib/mcclim/README`
  states that one file within it (`formula.lisp`, by Andy Hefner) is
  public domain.
* `share/contrib` itself (81 entries at this version) is a large,
  heterogeneously-authored grab-bag of individually-contributed
  packages, of which the two just above are the only ones this sweep's
  grep matched -- but given its size and provenance it is the single
  most likely place for further undisclosed license variance, and
  wasn't checked file-by-file below the directory level.

None of this changes the license terms this package itself redistributes
Maxima under (Maxima's own top-level COPYING, GPL-2, is still what this
package's own build treats as covering the combined work, matching how
Maxima itself ships) -- every exception found above is more permissive
than GPL-2 (BSD-style or public domain), which is fine to include in a
GPL-2 work, not a conflict with it. The point of recording them here is
so they're a known, named list instead of an open-ended "might be
something in there" -- and so a future version bump re-runs the same
three-step sweep above rather than assuming nothing changed.
